# ===========================================================================
#  Prebuilt llama.cpp binaries living in the ROOT of this folder.
#
#  These are the shipped Windows CUDA build: 33 DLLs (~668 MB, cublasLt64_13
#  alone is 460 MB) and 22 EXEs. They are redistributable artifacts, not
#  source, and must never enter git history -- once committed, that 668 MB is
#  permanent even if a later commit deletes it.
#
#  The leading "/" anchors each pattern to THIS directory only, so nothing
#  inside the llama.cpp submodule is affected (a submodule is a separate repo
#  with its own rules regardless, but anchoring keeps the intent explicit).
# ===========================================================================
/*.dll
/*.exe
/*.lib
/*.exp
/*.pdb
/*.ilk
/*.obj

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Local build trees, if you ever compile here or alongside the submodule.
#
#  ANCHORED WITH "/" ON PURPOSE. These were once unanchored, and git matches
#  an unanchored pattern at EVERY depth -- so "build-*/" silently swallowed
#  wiki/benchmarks/build-experiment/, the entire evidence trail for chapter 14,
#  which "git status" then reported as nothing to commit. This is the same trap
#  CLAUDE.md records for llama.cpp's own "/build*" eating a BUILD-* manifest.
#  Build trees only ever appear at the repo root or inside the submodule (which
#  has its own rules), so anchoring loses nothing and cannot hide evidence.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/build/
/build-*/
out/
cmake-build-*/
install/
.cache/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Model weights. Never commit these -- a single GGUF here is 15-20 GB.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.gguf
*.gguf.*
*.safetensors
*.bin
*.pt
*.onnx

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Benchmark artifacts.
#
#  ALL COMMITTED ON PURPOSE -- this is the evidence behind every number in the
#  wiki, and without it the tables are just assertions:
#    wiki/benchmarks/results.tsv        every throughput run, append-only
#    wiki/benchmarks/needle-results.md  long-context quality-gate results
#    wiki/benchmarks/mtp-results.md     speculative-decoding results
#    wiki/benchmarks/logs/              per-run llama.cpp output
#    wiki/benchmarks/needle-prompt.txt  the 404 KB synthetic haystack (INPUT)
#
#  The haystack is committed even though needle-test.ps1 regenerates it
#  deterministically (verified: identical SHA256). Two reasons it must be the
#  committed artifact rather than a regenerated one:
#    1. Line-ending normalisation would silently change the token count, so
#       "107,732 tokens" would stop reproducing. See .gitattributes.
#    2. Every configuration must be compared against a byte-identical prompt;
#       committing it removes any doubt that they were.
#
#  Only genuinely unbounded output is ignored: the background server log grows
#  for as long as a server runs.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
wiki/benchmarks/server.log
bench-results/

# Repo-local Codex discovery links. On Windows these are directory junctions into
# .claude/skills; committing them would duplicate the linked trees with core.symlinks=false.
/.agents/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Windows cruft. This folder USED to live inside OneDrive, which dropped
#  desktop.ini files around and created conflicted copies. It now lives at
#  S:\OsDevelop\llamacpp, outside any sync root -- the rules are kept anyway:
#  harmless, and they still apply if it is ever copied back under one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
desktop.ini
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
$RECYCLE.BIN/
*.lnk
*.tmp
*~
*-conflicted copy*
